{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>Like one who removes a garment</b> — the Targum [renders:] 'and he should not put upon him a cooling [draft] on a cold day.'" "<b>And vinegar on natron</b> — they resemble each other, likewise 'one who sings songs of joy upon a broken heart.' Natron is known as a cleaning agent; if water dissolves it, all the more so vinegar. That is, it removes or strips his garments from him, and a sick person is compared to both." "<b>And the word רע</b> — from תרועמה, meaning broken. And this is the way of rebuke: that the sick person not be angered, nor burdened with his [the visitor's] songs and words."], :book :proverbs, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {0 "The Targum of Proverbs 25:20 renders the Hebrew differently than the Masoretic text; Ibn Ezra quotes the Aramaic and paraphrases it in Hebrew."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Proverbs -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/mishley/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:24:42.629661343Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}